## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical NUMA-aware memory management, race-free migration, and measurement under contention.

## Key Concepts

- NUMA locality
- Page migration and pte updates
- Atomic synchronization and CAS
- TLB shootdown and cache coherence
- Contention control and testing

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
  int node;
  atomic_int heat;
  atomic_bool migrating;
} page_meta_t;

void try_migrate(page_t *p, int new_node) {
  if (atomic_load(&p->migrating)) return;
  if (atomic_exchange(&p->migrating, true)) return;
  // update pte, migrate data to new_node
  atomic_store(&p->node, new_node);
  // flush/invalidate as needed
  atomic_store(&p->migrating, false);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale migrations across thousands of pages without starving local allocations?
- How would you instrument and validate latency vs. throughput under synthetic CPU/memory-bound workloads?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of practical I/O scheduling fairness in a constrained, beginner-friendly setting.

## Key Concepts
- I/O scheduling fairness and starvation avoidance
- Per-process credit system and aging
- Fixed-size request queue management
- Enqueue/dequeue logic and safe selection

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct IOReq {
  int pid;
  uint64_t lba;
  int size;
  int read; // 1 = read, 0 = write
  int credit; // dynamic, per-process
  int age;    // waiting cycles
  int arrival;
} IOReq;

int pick_next(IOReq q[], int n) {
  int best = -1;
  int best_prio = -1;
  for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (q[i].arrival) {
      int prio = q[i].credit + (q[i].age > AGE_THRESHOLD ? 1 : 0);
      if (prio > best_prio) {
        best_prio = prio;
        best = i;
      }
    }
  }
  return best;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test starvation under a mixed workload (CPU-bound vs IO-bound)?
- How would you handle I/O errors and partial completions in this scheduler?

